it will be recommended recommended at the Monday's meeting that we will adopt the resolution next item please madam clerk number 20 resolution adopting a revised Cheyenne Larmy County Board of Health fee schedule for various services and inspections staff report please you have like a bag of name tags staff report please good morning Mr chair members of the committee i'm Kathy Emmens i'm the in this capacity I'm here as the executive director of the Cheyenne Laramie County Public Health so um what we are bringing to you today is a request for our increase of fees by statute we need to have both the county and the city approve our any fee increase in environmental health and

I'm also providing an updated handout so um in the P we've not raised fees since 2021 traditionally fees have been a flat rate across the board for every um fee that we provide or that for every service we provide and just to kind of give you a snapshot our fees include inspections of restaurants massage body art um all food establishments whether they be grocery store mini mart um full service restaurants we also do the small wastewater program which um is specific to septics and well inspections and you can see on the list that I gave you on the sheet I gave you all of those so I was challenged by the mayor two years ago to determine what it actually costs to do service rather than just a flat rate um

increase we took one year to do this we started this process in March of 2024 and um I just wanted to quickly review how we did that so you have a better feel for how we made these determinations so the first thing we did was to determine the hourly cost of service provision this included personnel software office and lab supplies training travel utilities postage indirect cost everything that it costs to do um to do our business and we arrived at a rate of $85 an hour the second thing we did was to research what others in the area are doing so Colorado Nebraska and Wyoming how they were charging for environmental health inspections and it was pretty much all

over the place there was no consistent um fees some were higher some were lower the next thing we did was to determine how much time each of the inspection activities took um and to be completed by the staff and then we multiplied that times the $85 an hour so it's based on actual time spent to conduct the service we examined then we examined an appropriate cost sharing rate between the private business or homeowners and the county city county fees that we get um from obviously from the city and the county we looked at everything from a 50% split up to a 100% split what we settled on and when I say we I mean our board of um

the Cheyenne Larmy County Board of Health um we made uh the calculations all the way from 50 to 100 we settled on a 60% owner split 40% city county contribution so cost of doing business we charge 60% the city and county makes up that additional 40% we then analyzed the existing fees and compared it with the new fee estimation based on the actual cost of doing business if the new rate was below the calculation based on the 60% not 100% um we didn't do anything if it was higher than the calculation we raised those rates to the 60% of the actual cost excuse me um this resulted in increases in 15 inspection areas ranging from $16 to $95 the 95 was for a multi-enue store like a big grocery store um

we uh we then modified the plan review structure so that it's based on again actual time spent doing a review rather than just a flat rate but that's a new a new way of doing business the back page of the handout I gave you shows all the fees that did not increase far more did not increase than actually did um let's see we completed we've completed the 45day time period that was published we received no comments from anyone um in the city or the county and the Laramie County Commissioners unanimously approved the fee increases on May 6 2025 so now we are bringing those to the city for review and and hopeful approval um from this body and

